          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.085,
            0.449,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(vi) Try this now: GH × H = 9K."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.111,
            0.848,
            0.185
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This means a 2-digit number multiplied by a 1-digit number gives another 2-digit number in the 90s. Observe the letters corresponding to the units digits in this cryptarithm. Pick the solution to this question from the options given below:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.185,
            0.845,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11 × 9 = 99, 12 × 8 = 96, 46 × 2 = 92, 24 × 4 = 96, 47 × 2 = 94, 31 × 3 = 93, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "16 \\times 6 = 96."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.232,
            0.513,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(vii) Here is one more: BYE × 6 = RAY."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.258,
            0.748,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Anshu says, “Since the product is a 3-digit number, B can’t be 2 or more. If B = 2, i.e., 2 hundreds, the product will be more than 1200. So, B = 1.”"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.764,
            0.204,
            0.928,
            0.355
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.764,
                0.204,
                0.928,
                0.355
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_20_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.324,
            0.747,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What can you say about ‘Y’? What digits are possible/not possible?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.367,
            0.847,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "“Y cannot be 7 or more because, if Y = 7, then 170 × 6 = 1020; but we want a 3-digit product. Also, Y will be even”, Anshu explains."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.405,
            0.847,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can solve cryptarithms using patterns, properties, and reasoning related to numbers and operations."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.452,
            0.349,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Solve the following:"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.477,
            0.361,
            0.495
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) UT × 3 = PUT"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.496,
            0.347,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) XY × 4 = ZX"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.477,
            0.565,
            0.495
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) AB × 5 = BC"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.404,
            0.496,
            0.594,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(v) PP × QQ = PRP"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.638,
            0.477,
            0.843,
            0.516
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) L2N × 2 = 2NP (vi) JK × 6 = KKK"
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.529,
            0.292,
            0.552
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.554,
            0.846,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. If 31z5 is a multiple of 9, where z is a digit, what is the value of z? Explain why there are two answers to this problem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.603,
            0.848,
            0.679
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. “I take a number that leaves a remainder of 8 when divided by 12. I take another number which is 4 short of a multiple of 12. Their sum will always be a multiple of 8”, claims Snehal. Examine his claim and justify your conclusion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.688,
            0.848,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. When is the sum of two multiples of 3, a multiple of 6 and when is it not? Explain the different possible cases, and generalise the pattern."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.754,
            0.847,
            0.793
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Sreelatha says, “I have a number that is divisible by 9. If I reverse its digits, it will still be divisible by 9”."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.797,
            0.784,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Examine if her conjecture is true for any multiple of 9."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.82,
            0.847,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Are any other digit shuffles possible such that the number formed is still a multiple of 9?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.867,
            0.848,
            0.905
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. If 48a23b is a multiple of 18, list all possible pairs of values for a and b."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.08,
            0.915,
            0.126,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": [
